All right, the recent changes to the HUD and main UI introduced visual assets for weapon cards. In `hud.js` I altered the buy‑card markup to include a `<span class="buy-art">` whose background image is set to `assets/weapons/${key}_card.png`. That means the browser now expects a CSS class named `buy-art` that will render the image correctly, size it, and give it a nice glow to match the neon aesthetic of the game.

I’ve already got a rule for `.buy-card:hover` in `style.css`, but there’s no definition for `.buy-art` yet. Without it, the `<span>` will just be an empty inline element, and the background image won’t be visible or sized properly. I need to add a new CSS block that:

1. Makes the element a block (so width/height apply).
2. Gives it a fixed height that matches the card art (the assets are 64 px tall, so `height: 64px`).
3. Adjusts margins to align the art nicely inside the card – a small negative margin on top/left/right to let the image bleed to the edges, and a tiny bottom margin for spacing.
4. Sets `background-size: contain` so the whole image fits, `background-repeat: no-repeat` to avoid tiling, and `background-position: center` to center it.
5. Adds a subtle neon‑styled drop‑shadow filter (`drop-shadow(0 0 6px rgba(61,255,200,0.25))`) to make the art pop against the dark UI, consistent with the rest of the neon theme.

The simplest way to inject this new rule is to edit `style.css` at the location of the existing `.buy-card:hover` rule. I’ll replace that exact snippet with itself plus the new `.buy-art` block appended directly after it. Since the file already contains the hover rule, I can use a non‑global edit (`replace_all: false`) targeting that exact string, ensuring I don’t accidentally modify any other similar rule.

Thus, the edit operation will replace:

```
.buy-card:hover { border-color: var(--neon); background: rgba(26, 36, 56, 0.95); }
```

with the same line followed by the new `.buy-art` definition:

```
.buy-card:hover { border-color: var(--neon); background: rgba(26, 36, 56, 0.95); }
.buy-art {
  display: block; height: 64px; margin: -4px -4px 2px;
  background-size: contain; background-repeat: no-repeat; background-position: center;
  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 6px rgba(61,255,200,0.25));
}
```

That should give the weapon cards their intended artwork and visual flair without disturbing any other styles. Proceed with the edit.

